A false claim is disproved by a counterexample: $n = 3$ disproves that $n^2$ being a multiple of 9 implies $n$ is a multiple of 9. A purported argument may instead establish only the converse of the claimed statement, which proves nothing about the claim itself. In proof by contradiction, one assumes the contrary of what is to be shown and deduces a contradiction, so the assumption must be false.
